course: biology
lesson: 148
title: Spider-Man: real spider senses, climbing and bites
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Peter Parker is bitten by a spider and gets climbing, a «spider sense» and super strength. A real bite gives nobody powers, but real spiders do have amazing versions of all three.
explain: A spider climbs glass because each foot has thousands of tiny hairs that touch the surface so closely that very small forces between molecules add up and hold it, the same trick a gecko uses.
explain: Its «spider sense» is real too: fine leg hairs bend with the smallest puff of air, telling the spider which way a fly is coming, and web spiders feel a trapped insect from a falling leaf.
explain: Watch a jumping spider on a windowsill: it turns, looks right at you with two big front eyes, ties on a safety thread and leaps onto a fly many times its own length away.
fact: Many spiders walk up glass and across ceilings using thousands of tiny foot hairs that grip by small forces between molecules; geckos use the same trick.
fact: Fine hairs on spiders' legs sense the tiniest air movements and tell the spider which way they come from.
fact: Web spiders feel vibrations so well they can tell a trapped insect from a leaf or a raindrop.
fact: Jumping spiders hunt by sight with two big front eyes; many see colour, and their main eyes can swivel inside the head.
fact: Jumping spiders leap many times their body length by pushing blood into their back legs, and tie on a silk safety line first.
fact: A spider bite cannot give anyone powers; most spider venom is made for killing insects, not people, so most bites are harmless or cause a little swelling, and only a few kinds of spider are dangerous to people, with good medicines for those bites.
fact: Tarantulas are mostly gentle; their bite is usually no worse than a bee sting, and some flick itchy hairs to defend themselves.
fact: Most spiders have eight eyes, but many see poorly and rely on touch and vibration; some cave spiders have no eyes at all.
fact: Spiders struggle on very smooth, wet surfaces, so a spider in the bath usually fell in or came from outside, not up the drain.
fact: Spiders in homes eat flies, mosquitoes and other pests, and all the spiders in the world eat hundreds of millions of tonnes of insects a year.
fact: Small animals are strong for their size, but a spider blown up to human size would struggle to hold up its own weight, because weight grows much faster than muscle strength.
fact: A wolf spider mother carries her egg sac, and when the babies hatch they ride on her back for days, sometimes over a hundred of them.
exercise: How can a spider climb straight up glass? | Thousands of tiny foot hairs grip by very small forces | It has round suckers on the bottom of its feet, like an octopus | It glues each foot down with silk | It is so light that it cannot fall
exercise: Which other animal climbs glass with the same trick? | The gecko | The garden snail | The common frog | The squirrel
exercise: What is a real spider's «spider sense»? | Fine leg hairs that feel the tiniest air movements | A sixth sense that lets it see what will happen in the future | Special ears hidden in its head | A magic tingle in its back
exercise: How does a web spider know a fly is trapped and not a leaf? | It feels the different vibrations in the web | It smells the fly from far away | It hears the fly's wings humming | It waits to see if the thing moves away
exercise: How does a jumping spider hunt? | By sight, stalking and pouncing on insects | By building a large sticky web | By waiting in a deep burrow | By smell, tracking insects for hours
exercise: How does a jumping spider power its jump? | It pushes blood into its back legs to straighten them | It winds up its legs like springs | It flaps its two front legs like wings to lift itself into the air | It pushes off with its silk thread
exercise: What does a jumping spider do before it leaps? | Ties on a silk safety line | Closes all of its eyes | Spins round three times | Eats a small snack
exercise: Can a spider bite give someone superpowers? | No, a bite cannot change a person's body like that | Yes, but only bites from rare spiders that live in faraway jungles | Yes, if the spider is radioactive | Only at night during a full moon
exercise: What does most spider venom target? | Insects, not people | Large animals like cows | Plants and green leaves | Only other spiders, nothing else
exercise: What is a tarantula bite usually like? | No worse than a bee sting | Deadly within a few minutes | Painless, like a soft pinch | Worse than any snake bite there is
exercise: How do some tarantulas defend themselves? | By flicking tiny itchy hairs | By spitting sticky silk | By squirting hot venom | By shouting a loud hiss
exercise: How many eyes do most spiders have? | Eight | Two | Six | Twelve
exercise: Why can't a spider climb out of a smooth, wet bath? | Water stops its tiny hairs from gripping well | Spiders hate getting their legs warm | It is too tired from crawling up the drain | It likes the warm bath and stays there on purpose to have a swim
exercise: Why are spiders useful in a house? | They eat flies, mosquitoes and other pests | They clean the dust and crumbs off the walls and the ceilings | They keep the mice and rats away from the house | They make the house warmer with their thick webs
exercise: Would a spider the size of a person be super strong? | No, it would struggle to hold up its own weight | Yes, it could lift a whole house | Yes, it could lift a hundred cars | No, it would be only just as strong as a fly, no matter how big it grew
exercise: What does a wolf spider mother do with her babies? | Carries them on her back for days | Leaves them in a web to fend for themselves | Carries them about in her mouth all day | Hides them under a stone for a year
test: «2 spiders on the ceiling, how many eyes are watching me then» | 16 | 4 | 8 | 12
test: «Peter got bitten and got powers, so if a spider bites me I'll get powers right» What is right? | No, a bite cannot give powers; most bites are harmless or just swell a little | Yes, if you get bitten by enough spiders | Yes, but only from a tarantula | No, because spiders cannot bite people at all; their fangs are much too soft
test: In a cartoon a spider lifts a truck. What is real? | Spiders are strong for their size, but a giant spider would struggle with its own weight | Spiders really can lift trucks | Spiders are weaker than any other animal | Spiders get stronger and stronger the bigger they grow, with no limit at all
test: «spider came up the plughole again, gross» What is more likely? | It fell in or came from outside and couldn't climb out of the slippery bath | Spiders live down in the drains and climb up the pipes every night to drink | The water pushes them up the pipes | Spiders are attracted to bath water
test: What would happen if a jumping spider forgot its safety line and missed the fly? | It would fall with nothing to stop it | It would fly back up | It would land on the fly anyway | Its web would catch it automatically
test: «keeper's got a massive hairy tarantula on her hand, she's mad, it'll kill her» What is right? | Tarantulas are mostly gentle; a bite is usually like a bee sting | Yes, a tarantula bite kills in seconds | Yes, tarantulas jump at every hand they see and bite it as hard as they can | No, tarantulas have no fangs at all
test: «spiders have 8 eyes so they must see amazing» What is right? | Many see poorly and rely on touch and vibration | Yes, all spiders see better than eagles | Yes, they can see in every direction perfectly, even in the dark | No, spiders have no eyes
test: A fly buzzes behind a spider and the spider turns to face it without seeing it. How? | Its leg hairs felt the air moving | It saw the fly in a mirror | It smelled the fly's breath | It guessed by luck
test: «wolf spider ran past with 100 babies on its back, about 5 fell off, how many left» | 95 | 105 | 100 | 5
test: «just squash every spider in the house, they're useless» Why might that be a bad idea? | Spiders eat flies, mosquitoes and other pests | Spiders keep the house cool by fanning their legs | Spiders keep the dust off by eating it from the floor | Spiders scare away burglars by building webs across the door
test: A jumping spider follows your finger with its front eyes without turning its body. How? | Its main eyes can swivel inside its head | It has eyes on stalks like a snail | It is listening, not looking | It moves its whole head in a circle
exam: Identify the mechanism by which many spiders adhere to vertical surfaces of glass. | Numerous minute hairs upon the feet exploit very small attractive forces between molecules | Suction cups upon the feet in the manner of an octopus | The deposition of adhesive silk upon each footstep | The negligible weight of the animal, which precludes any possibility of falling from a surface
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the bite of a spider in relation to human beings. | Such bites are in most cases harmless and cannot confer any extraordinary capacity | Such bites invariably confer exceptional strength and agility upon the person bitten | Such bites are in all cases fatal | Spiders are anatomically incapable of biting
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the sense by which a spider detects nearby movement of air? | Fine hairs upon the legs register minute currents of air and their direction | A sixth sense enables the spider to foresee danger | Concealed ears within the head receive the faint sound of anything that approaches the animal | The spider detects such movement solely by sight
exam: Ascertain the consequence of enlarging a spider to the dimensions of a human being. | The animal would struggle to support its own weight, strength increasing more slowly than weight | The animal would be capable of lifting buildings | The animal's strength would increase in exact proportion to its weight, so it would remain equally capable | The animal would become capable of flight
